# Day 6 — Server Security

## Goal

Harden the Raspberry Pi server by configuring a firewall, auditing open ports and services, enabling automatic security updates, securing SSH, and establishing a non-root administration workflow.

## What I did

* Identified the operating system and kernel
* Verified the normal administration user
* Audited listening network ports
* Audited enabled and running services
* Installed and configured UFW
* Denied incoming connections by default
* Allowed outgoing connections by default
* Allowed SSH through the firewall
* Enabled UFW
* Verified firewall status
* Verified open ports after enabling the firewall
* Installed `unattended-upgrades`
* Verified automatic package-list updates
* Verified automatic unattended upgrades
* Verified Debian security repositories
* Disabled automatic reboot after updates
* Audited potentially unnecessary services
* Retained required services for the current server setup
* Hardened SSH authentication
* Disabled root SSH login
* Disabled password authentication
* Enabled public-key SSH authentication
* Verified SSH configuration syntax
* Reloaded SSH safely
* Tested SSH access from the Mac after hardening
* Confirmed `.local` SSH access through Avahi

